//
// Expected: f1f0 — a share is exactly as fresh as the secret it was split
// from. SHAMIR_SPLIT is a multi-output primitive, so its outputs carry an
// output index alongside the argument, and this is the model that asserts the
// freshness descent looks THROUGH that index at the argument rather than
// treating a derived output as a value in its own right.

//
// The wire values are guarded, and that is not decoration. Under an active
// attacker an unguarded value is one the attacker may replace, so the
// recipient's state resolves it to the bare constant rather than to whatever
// the sender computed — and a bare constant transitively contains nothing at
// all, fresh or otherwise. Ungarding any message here turns every verdict
// below into f1 for a reason that has nothing to do with what this model is
// about. freshness_unguarded_wire.vp is where that effect is the subject.

attacker[active]

principal Dealer[
	knows private static_secret
	generates fresh_secret
	static_one, static_two, static_three = SHAMIR_SPLIT(static_secret)
	fresh_one, fresh_two, fresh_three = SHAMIR_SPLIT(fresh_secret)
]

Dealer -> Bob: [static_one], [fresh_one]

principal Bob[
	_ = HASH(static_one, fresh_one)
]

queries[
	freshness? static_one
	freshness? fresh_one
]
